#! /bin/bash
#
# tools/check_qt_namespace — CI guard for the static (QT_NAMESPACE) build.
#
# Fails if a Qt type (a class/struct forward declaration named Q*) is not
# enclosed in QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE/QT_END_NAMESPACE, or if that enclosure is
# unbalanced.
#
# Why: the plugin artifacts are built against a static Qt configured with
# QT_NAMESPACE (see the Justfile `plugin` flow). That Qt's headers do
# `using namespace ::<QT_NAMESPACE>;`, so Qt names resolve unqualified as
# usual — but a bare `class QFoo;` at any other scope collides with the
# namespaced real class under that using-directive (ambiguity at global
# scope, a wrong incomplete type inside a project namespace). The regular
# (system Qt) build compiles all of it fine, so neither its compilation
# nor its test run ever sees the breakage — only the static build does.
# That is exactly how a bare `class QProcess;` in H2PlayerTest.h broke the
# `just plugin` build while every regular CI job stayed green.
# QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE/QT_END_NAMESPACE is a no-op when QT_NAMESPACE is
# undefined, so the wrap is the one form correct for every build.
#
# Scope: every forward declaration (class/struct Q*;) under src/, plus the
# balance of the QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE/QT_END_NAMESPACE enclosure (an
# unclosed QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE is equally invisible to the regular build).
# Friend declarations and enum-class forward declarations do not introduce
# the name into the enclosing scope and are not matched.
#
# Legitimate exceptions are listed in ALLOW with a reason. Prefer the
# QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE/QT_END_NAMESPACE wrap over adding an entry here.
#
